What is the change in equilibrium concentration of a complex ion (FeCN$_6^{4-}$) in a 1.0 M iron (III) chloride solution when 0.25 moles of potassium cyanide (KCN) is added? The K$_{eq}$ value for the formation of FeCN$_6^{4-}$ is 1.2 x 10$^{20}$.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the change in equilibrium concentration of FeCN$_6^{4-}$ when KCN is added to an 1.0 M iron (III) chloride solution, we can use the concept of equilibrium and the given K$_{eq}$ value.

First, let's write the balanced chemical equation for the formation of the compound:

\[
\text{Fe}(3) + 6\text{NaCN} \rightarrow \text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4+}
\]

Now, let's set up the balanced chemical equation for the formation of FeCN$_6^{4-}$:

\[
\text{Fe}(3) + 6\text{CN}_4^- \rightarrow \text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4+}
\]

Given:
- Initial mass of KCN added = 0.25 moles
- M$_{Fe}(3) = 1.0$ M
- K$_{eq} = 1.2 \times 10^{20}$

Since KCN is added, we can infer that the concentration of CN$_4^-$ will increase by 0.25 moles. To find the change in concentration, we need to know the volume of the solution. However, since the volume is not given, we will assume the 0.25 moles of KCN are added to a volume of 1000 mL (1.0 M x 1000 mL = 1000 mL).

The change in concentration of KCN added is:
\[
\Delta M_{CN_4^-} = \frac{0.25 \text{ moles}}{1000 \text{ mL}} = 0.25 \text{ M}
\]

Now, we can set up the equilibrium table:

\[
\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\quad \text{Fe}(3) & + 6\text{CN}_4^- & \leftarrow & \text{Fe(CN)}_6^{4+} \\
\hline
\text{Initial Mass} & 1.0 & 0.25 & 0 \\
\hline
\text{Change} & -x & -6x & x \\
\hline
\text{Final Mass} & 1.0 - x & 0.25 - 6x & x \\
\hline
\end{array}
\]

Using the equilibrium ratio:

\[
K_{eq} = \frac{\left[\text{Fe}(CN)_6^{4+}\right]}{\left[\text{Fe}(3)\right] \left[\text{CN}_4^-\right]^6}
\]

Substituting the final values:

\[
