## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
a walks at 10 kmph and 3 hours after his start , b cycles after him at 20 kmph . how far from the start does b catch up with a ?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Let's denote the time it takes for B to catch up with A as \( t \) hours after B starts cycling.

When B starts cycling, A has already been traveling for 3 hours at a speed of 10 kmph. Therefore, the distance A has traveled when B starts is:

\( \text{Distance covered by A} = \text{Speed of A} \times \text{Time A has been traveling} = 10 \text{ km/h} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km} \)

Now, B starts cycling at a speed of 20 kmph. After \( t \) hours, B will have covered a distance of:

\( \text{Distance covered by B} = \20 \text{ km/h} \times t \text{ h} = 20t \text{ km} \)

Similarly, after \( t \) hours after B starts, A will have been traveling for \( t + 3 \) hours at a speed of 10 km/h. So, the distance A will have covered from the start is:

\( \text{Distance covered by A} = 10 \text{ km/h} \times (t + 3) \text{ h} = 10t + 30 \text{ km} \)

When B catches up with A, they will have covered the same distance from the starting point. Therefore, we can set the distances equal to each other:

\( 20t = 10t + 30 \)

Now, we can solve for \( t \):

\( 20t - 10t = 30 \)
\( 10t = 30 \)
\( t = 3 \) hours

So, B takes 3 hours to catch up with A after he starts cycling.

To find the distance from the start when B catches up with A, we can use the distance covered by B:

\( \text{Distance} = 20 \text{ km/h} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km} \)

So, B catches up with A $\boxed{60}$  hours from the start.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

