The period of sin(ωx + φ) is T = 2π/ω. In this case, ω = 2, so the period of 3sin(2x + π/4) is T1 = 2π/2 = π.

For the second term, 2cos(3x - π/3), the period is calculated as follows:
The period of cos(ωx + φ) is T = 2π/ω. In this case, ω = 3, so the period of 2cos(3x - π/3) is T2 = 2π/3.

Now, to find the period of the combined function, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the two periods T1 and T2.

T1 = π = π
T2 = 2π/3

The LCM of π and 2π/3 is the smallest value that is a multiple of both periods. We can find the LCM by multiplying the denominators of the fractions and dividing by the greatest common divisor of the numerators.

LCM(π, 2π/3) = (π * 2π/3) / (2π) = (2π^2/3) / (2π) = π/3

